[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky light ramp PUBLISHING to the marcher (Phase L2). sun/ambient tints by elevation: horizon(0) sun=1.25/0.72/0.42 amb=0.78/0.86/1.10 | noon(+60) sun=1.00/1.00/1.00 amb=1.00/1.04/1.12 | civil(-6) sun=0.70/0.72/0.95 amb=0.62/0.76/1.06 | night(-18, new moon) amb=0.55/0.68/1.00, full moon amb=0.80/0.91/1.18 | THIS FRAME alt=38.07 deg moonFrac=0.00000 sun=1.03/1.01/0.97 amb=0.98/1.03/1.12. Consumed only while voxel.March.VSLighting is 1; the noon row IS the marcher's shipped 1.00/1.04/1.12 ambient, so a noon frame is unchanged.
[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon DISC RESOLVED: 2.340 deg radius (4.68 deg across, 9.0x the real moon's 0.26), gain 0.150. At 2200x1300 / 90 deg FOV that is about 90 pixels wide. Written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky (MoonAngularRadius + MoonBrightness), so the asset defaults are dead -- voxel.Sky.MoonAngularRadiusDeg and voxel.Sky.MoonDiscBrightness are the knobs. These move the DISC ONLY; the ground is lit by voxel.Sky.MoonIntensity.
[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon has SET: altitude -36.65 deg, azimuth 342.8 deg, 99% illuminated (phase 0.530, 0=new 0.5=full) at game 12.00 h with the SUN at +38.07 deg. MoonEnabled=1, disc radius 2.340 deg, voxel.Sky.MoonEpochOffsetDays=+0.000. A moon BELOW the horizon or at ~0% illuminated is not a bug -- both draw nothing on purpose. At offset 0 the shipped epoch puts this crossing at wall-clock +29.3 min into every session, identically, because the clock is solved to the same 37800.000 s every run; a NEGATIVE offset brings it forward.
[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky star map handedness RESOLVED: StarUDirection=+1.0 (read back from /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky, raw +1.000), so StarRotation is being driven as +1.0 * (LST/360 + 0.0000 turns). Flipping the MPC scalar mirrors the map AND re-signs this drive together; driving +LST against a -1 map would rotate the sky backwards.
[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky StarAmbientGain RESOLVED to 1.0000, DERIVED from kStarAmbientCalibration (voxel.Sky.StarAmbientGain requested -1.0000; written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky). Derived means appearance and illumination cannot drift apart unnoticed; the constant was measured at voxel.Sky.StarGain 0.15 against a snowfield whose ground/sky luminance ratio came out 0.790, i.e. snow's albedo. This is the SkyLight capture's star gain ONLY -- it cannot change a main-view pixel, because M_SkyAtmosphereDome routes it through a ReflectionCapturePassSwitch. It is the RATIO of capture star radiance to visible star radiance: voxel.Sky.StarGain (0.1500 here) is already inside the shared StarBrightness scalar both domes multiply, so this number carries none of it. 0 removes starlight from the ambient term entirely. Re-measure with tools/sky-albedo-check.py on a starlit capture: ground/sky luminance must equal the ground's albedo.
[2026.09.07-16.50.15:775][998]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky forward-shading PRIMARY is now the SUN (sun altitude +38.07 deg, moon altitude -36.65 deg, moon intensity 0.0000). Read back: sun priority -1, moon priority -1 -- these MUST differ or the editor's 'multiple directional lights are competing' warning returns (LightGridInjection.cpp:1662). This is the ONE light that lights translucency, water and volumetric fog; handover at sun -6.0 deg with 1 deg of hysteresis, derived from kMoonSunSuppressStartDeg.
